## Collect a bounded report

Read the task's run and correctness contract before choosing a capture target. Run the capture command through your shell in the candidate's execution environment. The `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` server only reads reports and discovers NCU capabilities; it does not execute candidate code. Prefer a task-provided capture entry point that warms the candidate and marks the capture interval. Do not modify candidate source or the scored benchmark.

Choose one representative case, device, and kernel. Use a narrow kernel filter or launch count so NCU does not replay the full workload. Start with a `basic` set and save the resulting `.ncu-rep` in the writable profile artifact directory supplied by this run. For a task-provided executable, adapt this pattern to its documented arguments and replace the output placeholder with that directory:

```
ncu --target-processes all --set basic --kernel-name 'regex:<target>' --launch-count 1 --force-overwrite -o <profile-output-prefix> <task capture command>
```

If the target uses CUDA profiler start/stop markers, add `--profile-from-start off`. If NCU reports missing permissions, unsupported counters, no matching kernel, or an absent executable, report the diagnostic and stop. Do not invent metrics.

## Analyze

Call `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` tools `capabilities`, `list_sections_and_sets`, `query_metrics(substring)`, and `read_report(path, page, contains)` as needed. Supported pages are `details`, `raw`, `source`, and `session`. Use `contains` to select metric names or kernel names from a large report. The reader returns bounded output and identifies truncation. Use `raw` for specific counters after reading `details`; narrow and repeat the capture when a broad report omits needed metrics. Retain the `.ncu-rep` path, selected kernel, case, collection set, device, and command in your analysis so the finding is reproducible.

NCU kernel timing is perturbed by replay and counter collection. Use the task's trusted unprofiled benchmark for `perf_metric`; set it to null when unavailable. Do not infer an end-to-end speedup directly from one kernel's profiled timing. State what evidence supports the bottleneck and what remains uncertain.

